Title: Agents poll with sleep while waiting for background jobs: verify what is in place, then close the gap
Created: 2026-10-25
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-10-25T06:26Z claude created
2026-10-25T06:28Z reviewer claude note Evidence from arena leg 1372-f1m (cycle 4, 2026-10-24), Pi session read call by call: 2 668 s of sleep in 11 waits. Two were pgrep waiters of the 0889 shape and ran to their iteration cap: 'for i in 1..20; pgrep -f "dvc pull"; sleep 15' lasted 301 s although dvc pull had finished within 30 s, and 'for i in 1..30; pgrep -f check1372.sh; sleep 20' lasted 601 s although the third gate run (wall 272 s) was long done: the waiting shell matches its own command line, so the loop never sees the job end. That is 902 s, 34% of the sleep. The other nine waits were fixed sleeps (5 to 420 s) set without knowing the job length; e.g. sleep 420 on a 276 s gate run. Nothing ended the long waits except the loop cap or the timer.

--- body ---
## Context

Agents wait for background jobs by looping on `sleep`, and the time is large. Measured on the arena (ticket 1094,
cycle 4, 2026-10-24, derived from Pi session timestamps by `scripts/leg-timing.py`): leg `1372-f1m` took 3 787 s of which
2 668 s were `sleep` (70 %); `1372-f1x` took 1 833 s with 1 126 s of `sleep`. The earlier handoff recorded `sleep` as 7.8 % of local
leg wall-clock. These are polling loops on background test runs, not the model's speed.

## What was found before writing this (to be confirmed in the first step)

- Ticket 0889 (2026-09-10) added a PreToolUse guard against shell loops that poll `pgrep` for a process: 105 such waiters over
  551 transcripts, two stranded processes. The guard `scripts/guard-pgrep-waiter.sh` no longer exists: commit 90a62683
  (ticket 0976) deleted guards, its title reading "the eight PreToolUse guards that never earned their place". It covered `pgrep` loops, not `sleep` polling on a log.
- The portable-wait design of 2026-10-21 (journal `2026-10-21-await-external-probes.md`; PRs #1542, #1552; ticket 1163; the
  stopguard design, ticket 1164, deferred) chose the instruction route first: each runtime's own background facility with a deadline.
- The snapshot pinned for the arena night (`night-pins/2026-10-24-c4-r1/payload`, the Pi agent directory) contains no wait or
  sleep rule that I could find by grepping `pgrep`, `sleep` and `background job`.

## First step

Verify, before deciding anything: does any rule or skill that reaches Pi tell the agent how to wait for a background job; what
do the long legs actually run (`sleep N` in a loop, on a log or on a process); which runtime facility could replace it
(a blocking run with a timeout, a notification when the job ends); and was the instruction route implemented for Pi or only
for Claude Code. Record the answer in the ticket log.

## Barriers to compare (a hard barrier, not a sign)

The sign has been tried: a memory note with the right recipe existed from 2026-09-09 and reached none of the 105 waiters (ticket
0889). The candidates below are walls. Each is to be discussed on three axes, and none is chosen here.

- **A. Fix the primitive.** Make the self-match impossible: a `pgrep`/`pkill` shim that excludes the caller's own process tree, or
  a `waitfor <pid|pattern|log-line> --timeout N` command that blocks until the job ends. Removes the cause for every caller.
  Open: a shim on PATH is bypassed by `/usr/bin/pgrep` or by `ps | grep`; a `waitfor` has to be known to the agent, so it still
  needs one line of instruction.
- **B. A hard cap per command in the tool layer.** The Bash call is killed after N seconds. Needs no cooperation from the agent.
  Open: it pushes legitimate long runs into the background, which is where the polling starts, so a cap without a blocking wait
  primitive may increase the polling. Which runtimes expose such a cap (a setting in Claude Code and Codex, an extension
  in Pi) is to be verified, not assumed.
- **C. A PreToolUse hook** that matches the shape of the command and denies it with a message that names the remedy (the 0889
  design). Open: command strings are a brittle surface (0889 needed three review rounds for `for` loops and heredocs); a hook
  costs one process per call; whether Pi can run one is to be verified from the 2026-10-21 probes.
- **D. An instruction.** The baseline that did not reach the 105; kept only as the line that teaches the primitive of A.

Axes to discuss, for each candidate:

- **Portability**: Claude Code, Codex and Pi, and the arena's pinned snapshot. A barrier that exists in one runtime and not in
  the one running the tournament's legs protects nothing there.
- **Efficiency**: tokens and latency per call, resident-prompt budget, and the hours it gives back (measured as in ticket 1169).
- **Specificity**: what it blocks that was legitimate. Each candidate ships with a replay over the trace corpus: would-fire
  count, a sample of the false positives, and a positive control (the lesson of 0976, which deleted eight guards for having
  no evidence they earned their place; this one was among them with 0 firings, never wired live).
- **Fairness in the arena**: any barrier changes what a leg measures. It enters the harness pin, applies to every arm, and is
  introduced between two series, not inside one.
- **Where the rails live for the arena**: a rail inside one harness protects only the arms that run that harness, so the
  arena keeps its own in the meta-harness (the runner and the leg environment), identical for every arm including the bare
  ones, with every firing logged and audited, and a run of the empty harness to read which rails fire even there (arena
  ticket 1114). A rail that fires on the empty harness is about the agent or the model; one that fires only inside IDH is about
  an IDH instruction, and that is where the harness-side barrier belongs.

Generalisation: ticket 1217 (trace-doctor prescribes barriers for the follies it finds). This ticket is its pilot case.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The finding above, confirmed or corrected, with the commands used
- [ ] A decision: close as already addressed (with the evidence), or the smallest hard barrier that reaches the Pi legs, chosen among A to C (D stays as the line that teaches A) with the three axes filled in from a replay
- [ ] Cite the bounded background waits already merged (`ce2965be`, `70b39b0a`, `6b39fd9c`) and say what they leave uncovered
- [ ] If a change is made, a re-measure of `sleep` share on a few legs after the change
